{
  "dom_id": "step-q335152",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2x+z-w,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=xz+y,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=y-z+{w}^{2},\\, & w(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(1)≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z(1)≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w(1)≈$\n{{field-6}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=-1,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=-1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+z-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+1-(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xz+y \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =y-z+{w}^{2} \\\\  & =0-1+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  | $-1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+2=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+0=1 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=(-1)+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2,$\n$z(1)≈1,$\nand\n$w(1)≈-1.$",
